  "query_preview": "..\n/Issued 1158 PM MDT Fri Jul 24 2026/ \n\n - Meteorological Overview:\n\nThe upper-level ridge will continue to bring heat impacts for the\nweekend along with afternoon showers and thunderstorms. Isolated\nshowers and thunderstorms continue to develop into the early\nmorning hours for Southwest MT. DCAPE values exceeding 1,500 J/kg\nwill keep an isolated strong wind threat with convection. This \nafternoon, a shortwave will develop isolated to scattered showers \nand thunderstorms for areas mainly along and east of the I-15 \ncorridor. 700mb winds increases throughout the day and forecast \nsoundings show deep inverted V profiles with 30-50kts of shear. \nDCAPE exceeding 1,000 J/kg will allow for thunderstorms to be \ncapable of producing strong to severe wind gusts. Forcing Sunday \nwill be a little bit weaker with isolated showers and \nthunderstorms developing again for Southwest and Central MT. The \nthreat for severe weather will be lower, but a very strong dry \nsurface layer will still allow for isolated strong winds to \ndevelop with showers and thunderstorms.\n\nHeat remains to be a concern through the weekend, especially\nacross North-Central MT. Saturday looks to be the hottest day\nstill, with afternoon high temperatures reaching into the low mid\n100s and overnight temperatures briefly cooling down to the mid\n60s to low 70s this morning. The Extreme Heat Warning remains \nvalid for today. Sunday won't be as hot, and model guidance has \ndown trended temperatures slightly. Though afternoon high \ntemperatures in the mid to upper 90s at lower elevations will \nstill bring moderate heat impacts. Sunday morning's temperatures \nlooks to have a longer period of cooling, as there will be around \na 6 hour period of temperatures dropping below 70 degrees. With \nheat impacts trending downward slightly Sunday due to more \ncooling in overnight temperatures, the Extreme Heat warning got \ntransitioned into a heat advisory for lower elevations in Cascade,\nChouteau, Hill, and Blaine Counties.\n\nThis ridging pattern looks to continue through next week.\nTemperatureswon't be as hot, but will remain above normal. \nModels are trending next week as drier, but with decent PWATs and \ndisturbances embedded win the ridge, I can't rule out isolated \ndaily chances of showers and thunderstorms. -Wilson",
